6: ## Q1 - the repair
7: 
8: **(a) CMMU selection.** READ. `cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? 0 : 1` matches `memory_m88k.c:112` and the device comments (S6). Callers that can reach the arm on these machines:
9: 
10: - Guest data `ld`/`st`/`xmem`, including `.usr`: `CACHE_DATA` or `CACHE_DATA | MEMORY_USER_ACCESS`. The mask `CACHE_FLAGS_MASK` (0x3) leaves `CACHE_DATA` (0), so index 1.
11: - Instruction fetch (`cpu_m88k_instr.c:2989-2990`) and the single-step pre-read (`cpu_dyntrans.c:287-288`): `CACHE_INSTRUCTION` (1), so index 0.
12: - Debugger examines and the disassembler look-behind pair `CACHE_NONE` or `CACHE_INSTRUCTION` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`. The arm's own guard (`memory_rw.c:603`) rejects them before the selector runs.
13: 
14: No caller that reaches the arm is misrouted. `CACHE_NONE` is not a separate reachable cell: every bare `CACHE_NONE` that can run here is paired with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and `dev_ether.c`'s bare `CACHE_NONE` is test-machine DMA into RAM, not a refusing device access. On luna-88k, `ncpus > 4` is `exit(1)` (`machine_luna88k.c:103-106`); cpus 0-3 each get both CMMUs (`dev_luna88k.c:934-965`, `:1074-1077`). The arm writes the faulting cpu's `cmmu[]`. `dev_m8820x.c:250-251` serves `d->cpu_nr` of the device the guest addresses. Those agree when the guest reads its own CMMU. The witness is cpu 0 only; `-n 2..4` stays NOT CLAIMED.
15: 
16: **(b) The two values.** READ for the encoding; the low two bits are NOT CHECKABLE from a local primary source. PFSR = `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR << 16` = `3 << 16` = `0x00030000` (`m8820x.h:123-125`). That is the field OpenBSD tests with `(pfsr >> 16) & 7`. A raw `3` fails it: `(3 >> 16) & 7` is 0. PFAR = `(uint32_t)orig_paddr`, the physical address saved at `memory_rw.c:437` before `:494` makes `paddr` device-relative (S1). **P2:** write it unmasked. Warrant: upstream `4e3e72b1` stores `orig_paddr` with no mask, and the MC88200 User's Manual, 2nd edition (not on disk; manuals.plus excerpts) says a bus-error PFAR holds the physical M-bus address of the transaction, while write-protection "destroys" PFAR, which is what this tree already does at `memory_m88k.c:399`. The P-bus presents A31-A2 plus byte enables. The retrieved excerpts have no PFAR bit diagram, so whether bits 1:0 are forced 0 is NOT CHECKABLE. Masking would be an extra invention. Every witness access is a word at a word-aligned address, so no row distinguishes the two. A sub-word row is clause (e), disclosed, not owed.
17: 
18: **(c) NULL scope.** READ. **P4 holds.** The check wraps only the two stores. `m88k_exception`, the `!no_exceptions` guard, the `debug()` line, and `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` stay outside it. Nothing in the ruled design moves them (Q1(d)).
19: 
20: No guest-reachable claimed refusal on a running CPU with a NULL `cmmu[]` slot exists on the default in-tree machines. MVME187 attaches both CMMUs only to `bootstrap_cpu` (`dev_mvme187.c:136-156`); `emul.c` starts that CPU and `machine_mvme88k.c` does not start a secondary. `-n 2` creates CPU 1 with NULL slots and does not run it. LUNA attaches both CMMUs to every CPU it starts. The 88110 machines and mvme188 attach none; barem88k, mvme188, and mvme197 install no devices. testm88k's only `return 0` is `dev_fb.c:719-721`, and `#182` keeps the mapped length equal to `framebuffer_size` (`dev_fb.c:190-212`), so a claimed access cannot reach it. `dev_fbctrl`'s own access returns 1.
21: 
22: The NULL check is licensed by transferred warrant (upstream r6577(e), and the same test at `memory_m88k.c:131-132`), not by a measured guest PFSR delta (R13). Removing it, or moving `m88k_exception` inside it, reds no row: both CMMUs exist on mvme187 and luna-88k. Disclose it. Do not row it. It is a host NULL dereference only if some later machine both runs a NULL-cmmu CPU and hits a claimed refusal.
23: 
24: **(d) Exception unchanged.** READ. The vector expression, the guard, the `debug()` line, and the failure return stay. The design adds two stores and a NULL test in front of the existing call.
25: 
26: **(e) LUNA return path.** READ. **P10.** `case 0x3ffffff0:` becomes `return 0;` and the PFSR store at `:626` is deleted. The case stays. Deleting it falls into `default:` (`:909-924`), which sets `cpu->running = 0`, and `memory_rw.c:591-592` then returns before the arm. `return 0` skips the common tail (`:927-930`), which only writes the already-zeroed read buffer. The load handler does not consume that buffer on failure (`cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c:189-195`).
27: 
28: Post-fix, a data load at exactly `0x3ffffff0`: the case does not clear `cpu->running`, so `:591-592` does not fire; `res` is forced from 0 to -1 (`:595-596`); the selector takes `cmmu[1]`; both registers are written; vector 3 is raised; the function returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED`. A store at that address takes the same metadata and the same vector; the case does not look at `writeflag`, and the stored data is discarded. An instruction fetch selects `cmmu[0]` and raises vector 2, then R1 stops the CPU before a guest handler runs. Any width whose start address is exactly `0x3ffffff0` hits the case; `0x3ffffff1` hits `default:` and stops the CPU. Direction is already pinned on mvme187 by `ust`. A LUNA store row is clause (e).
29: 
30: **(f) The build.** READ. **P1 is right, and it is load-bearing.** `tmp_m88k_tail.c:38-40` defines `MEM_M88K` and `#include`s `memory_rw.c` into `cpu_m88k.c`. `CMMU_PFSR`, `CMMU_PFAR`, and `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR` exist only in `thirdparty/m8820x.h`. That header is included by `memory_m88k.c`, `dev_m8820x.c`, `dev_luna88k.c`, and `dev_mvme187.c`, and by nothing under `src/cpus/`. `memory_m88k.c` is a separate object (`Makefile.skel:128-129`). As specified, the repair does not compile in the m88k translation unit.
31: 
32: Place the include in `cpu_m88k.c`, after the existing `#include "thirdparty/m8820x_pte.h"` (`cpu_m88k.c:53`). Do not put it in `tmp_m88k_tail.c`: that file is generated (`Makefile.skel:140-141`). Order matters because `m8820x.h`'s `NBSG` uses `PDT_BITS` and `PG_BITS` from `m8820x_pte.h`. The header's own `struct m8820x_cmmu` is inside `#if 0`; the live struct is `cpu_m88k.h:234-240`, and `reg[]` is 1024 `uint32_t`s, so index 67 (`0x10c / 4`) is in range. No token in `src/cpus/` uses `BATC8`, `BATC9`, `NBSG`, `INST_CMMU`, `DATA_CMMU`, `MAX_CMMUS`, or `CMMU_PFSR`/`CMMU_PFAR`. `m8820x_pte.h`'s `CACHE_INH`/`CACHE_WT`/`CACHE_GLOBAL`/`CACHE_MASK` are distinct from `memory.h`'s `CACHE_*`. No collision. `-Wall -Wextra -Wshadow` has no `-Wconversion`, so a bare `uint64_t` store into `uint32_t` would not warn; keep Grok's `(uint32_t)` cast anyway.
33: 
34: `cpu_m88k.c` joins the edit set and the S17 clearance. It is not in `tools/est_divergence.txt` and is byte-identical in `est/` (C0 part 5). The witness baseline's three-file list is wrong.
