149: ## Q4 - standing licence
150: 
151: **R1: file it. Do not merge it into T36, and do not block this round on it.** Free-running, a refused m88k instruction fetch takes vector 2 and then `cpu_m88k_instr.c:2989-2993` reaches the dyntrans `bad:` tail (`cpu_dyntrans.c:1983-2014`), which sets `cpu->running = 0` on the faulting CPU (`:2014`) and requests the debugger (`:2002`, `:2021`) before the handler's first instruction. "All machines stopped" in the transcript is the one-CPU consequence. It was not measured on a multi-cpu machine. The tail is generic dyntrans, so other architectures may do the same. Committed probes that key on the same message (`mips_subnorm_probe.py`, `ppc_halt_probe.py`, `sh_halt_probe.py`, and the alpha-ifetch artifacts) use it as a halt oracle on other ISAs. They do not depend on an m88k instruction-side PFSR. Severity is the project's MED-class wrong-result: guest recovery never runs. It is a prerequisite for a free-running observation of the `cmmu[0]` cell. It is not a prerequisite for this round's data-side fix. The ruling already orders T36 before later 88K fault rounds.
152: 
153: **R2: file it. It does not block T36.** The single-step disassembly pre-read is `CACHE_INSTRUCTION` without `NO_EXCEPTIONS` (`cpu_dyntrans.c:287-290`). When the debugger steps onto a refusing device, its own read raises the exception and changes guest-visible vector, PFSR, and PFAR state. A grep of `regress/**/*.py` found the "could not read the instruction" / "UNIMPLEMENTED instruction" strings used as halt oracles on MIPS, PowerPC, and SH, and in alpha-ifetch artifacts, plus this witness's unscored INFO line. No committed top-level m88k probe's rows were found to require that pre-read to raise. That is not a proof that none exist if a probe jumps the PC onto a refusing device. `vme_default_probe.py` steps data accesses whose instructions sit in RAM. R2 contaminates any stepped instruction-fetch row, which is why P5 refuses option (i). It does not contaminate the data rows.
154: 
155: **R3: do not file it in this round.** An unclaimed physical address is a different path (`memory_rw.c:662-713`, with the upstream TODOs at `:682` and `:709-710`): a read is served zeros, a write is dropped, no exception, no CMMU write, before and after this fix. The witness measured that (INFO `unclaimed`). `vmesbcp2.txt` lists local bus timeout, VME BERR*, and bus-error processing in its contents. The VSBchip2 LBTE text around line 3480 describes the VSBchip2 recording LST status when it is local-bus master. It does not say whether an unclaimed CPU-master local access asserts BERR*. Whether real MVME187 or LUNA hardware bus-errors there stays a board question. The CHANGELOG must not claim this fix covers it.
156: 
157: **What the drafter got wrong, loudly.**
158: 
159: 1. S16 / P1. The repair as written does not compile. `cpu_m88k.c` is in the edit set. The witness's three-file list is incomplete.
160: 2. P6's discharge sentence is false. A `NO_EXCEPTIONS` access reaches the guard and does not enter the body. Clause (e), with that reason.
161: 3. Section 7's floor includes the instruction label, the NULL cell, and `no_exceptions` set. Those are clause (e). The witness's rows are the floor.
162: 4. P7 understates reachability. A programmed BATC separates VA and PA. The hardwired BATC8/BATC9 entries do not. The APR check precedes BATC.
163: 5. The LUNA half on HEAD already writes PFSR. Do not describe it as "PFSR not written."
164: 6. The comment citation `dev_luna88k.c:614` is already stale. The write is at `:626`.
165: 7. Do not record the null medium as 0/24. An exit-1 empty stub is 1/24 with `CONTROL=FAIL`. An absent binary prints no sentinel.
166: 
167: S1-S4, S6, S8-S11, S13-S15, and S17-S19 stand. S5's caller list is right, and the selector does not misroute any caller that reaches the arm. S7's "no reachable NULL cell on an unmodified running guest" holds for the default machines, with the `-n 2` caveat that the extra MVME187 CPU exists and is not started. S12 stands: no MC88200 manual is on disk. The web excerpts support the unmasked PFAR choice and do not settle bits 1:0.

