## Q1 - repair: complete and correct?

**(a) Selector.**  
READ from S5/S6: all non-`NO_EXCEPTIONS` callers reaching the arm pass `CACHE_INSTRUCTION` (guest instruction fetch, single-step pre-read) or `CACHE_DATA` (loads, stores, xmem). `cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? 0 : 1` therefore routes correctly. Multi-cpu LUNA: the arm writes `cpu->cd.m88k.cmmu[...]` of the faulting cpu; dev_m8820x.c serves each CMMU from `d->cpu_nr` (S8), so a later guest read on that cpu sees the same CMMU. No misroute found.

**(b) PFSR and PFAR.**  
READ S8/S9: `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR << 16 = 0x00030000`; `orig_paddr` is physical (S1).  
P2 low bits: **NOT CHECKABLE** from packet. S12 says no MC88200 manual is on disk; S10 gives DMA0 as word address, but PFAR semantics after bus error are not established. Witness rows are aligned words only. A sub-word row would be clause-(e) debt, not floor (marginal of a single operand value, R7).

**(c) NULL check.**  
REASONED from S7/P4: no unmodified machine has a guest-reachable refused access on a cpu with a NULL `cmmu[]` slot. Testm88k's candidate is fenced; barem88k/mvme188/mvme197 install no devices. Therefore the NULL check is not reachable in current product. It is licensed as a transferred warrant from upstream's hunk (3.4). Variants removing/moving the NULL check are unobservable at rung 3 and must be disclosed under clause (e), not rowed.

**(d) Other arm elements.**  
READ from 3.1/3.3/8.1: vector expression, `!no_exceptions` guard, `debug()` line, and `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` all stay unchanged.

**(e) LUNA path.**  
READ S19/8.2: `return 0` skips the common tail; `cpu->running` stays true so :591-592 does not fire; `res` 0-1; `!no_exceptions` true; arm writes data CMMU and raises data-access exception vector 3.  
For a **store**: same path, cache `CACHE_DATA`, data-access exception.  
For an **instruction fetch** at 0x3ffffff0: selector writes cmmu[0] and raises instruction-access exception vector 2, but free-running still halts before the handler because of R1; stepped observation depends on R2. This is the P5 floor-cell problem.

**(f) P1 include.**  
READ S16/3.4: the repair as written uses `CMMU_PFSR`, `CMMU_PFAR`, `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR`, which live only in `thirdparty/m8820x.h`. That header is not included by `cpu_m88k.c` or `tmp_m88k_tail.c`. Therefore the m88k build will fail without adding `#include "thirdparty/m8820x.h"` to `cpu_m88k.c`. **cpu_m88k.c joins the edit set.** Collision with existing identifiers is **NOT CHECKABLE** from this packet, but upstream added the same include (3.4), which is strong evidence it compiles.

**(g) P9 stale comments.**  
READ P9: the TODO at memory_rw.c:615-619 is replaced by the new writes. The three dev_m8820x.c comments become stale and should be corrected in this diff; that makes dev_m8820x.c a comment-only edit site. Yes.

**(h) Scope of "88K bus errors".**  
REASONED from S3/S4: only claimed-and-refused accesses reach the arm. On mvme187 that is unreadable windows and VME population 1; on LUNA only the probe case. CHANGELOG/probe contract may claim exactly those, and must disclaim unclaimed addresses, instruction refusals free-running, sub-word accesses, translation-on, and NULL-CMMU configurations.

**(i) P3 sibling and P8 boot.**  
P3: `kept` is defensible. S9 shows translation faults reset the sibling; S11 shows OpenBSD branches on either PFSR being BERROR. A stale sibling BERROR can only persist from a previous bus error; a later non-bus-error translation fault resets it. No scenario in the packet shows a stale sibling changing a non-bus-error outcome. Warrant is partial; disclose limitation.  
P8: the LUNA change is user-visible. A before/after boot-to-`login:` measurement on the luna rig is owed as part of the full battery (gate 7), not necessarily as a targeted gate for this round.

**(j) LUNA disposition options.**  
READ 3.3/R12: option (2) `return 0` into the arm is correct. Options (1), (3), (4) are named. No further member is apparent from the packet.

