## Q1 -- the repair

**(a)** REASONED from S5/S6: the only callers that can reach the arm are guest data loads/stores/`xmem` (CACHE_DATA  cmmu[1], correct) and the instruction fetch plus the single-step pre-read (CACHE_INSTRUCTION  cmmu[0], correct -- both are instruction-side). Look-behind and debugger accesses carry NO_EXCEPTIONS and cannot reach the arm (READ). No misroute exists among the callers the packet enumerates. NOT CHECKABLE: whether any caller passes a cache class other than DATA/INSTRUCTION with no_exceptions clear -- settling fact: the flags at every MEMORY_RW call site. Multi-cpu luna is consistent for the rowed cpu-0 cells (arm writes the faulting cpu's cmmu; the device serves `d->cpu_nr`'s -- READ); whether every started luna cpu has a matching dev_m8820x instance is NOT CHECKABLE (settling fact: dev_luna88k.c's device-creation loop vs its cmmu-attach loop, :934-965 vs :1074-1077). Multi-cpu is disclosed (axis 14), so no row is at risk.

**(b)** PFSR 0x00030000: READ (S8 BERROR=3; translation path encodes `<<16`, S9; trap.c extracts `(>>16)&7`). Enumerated: 3<<16 = 0x30000; 0x30000>>16 & 7 = 3; the seeds do not collide with the wants (0x5a5a&7 = 2, 0x2468&7 = 0) -- the seed choice is sound. PFAR = orig_paddr: READ (S1: :437 saves, :494 subtracts; 8.1 places the arm after both) -- sound, and both seats' correction is consistent with the inlined code. P2: UNKNOWN on hardware warrant (READ: no MC88200 manual on disk; the MC88100 P-bus note is about the bus, not PFAR). Disposition: adopt upstream's byte-address choice as a transferred warrant (R13) and record it as such; all rowed addresses are word-aligned (0xfff46010, 0xfff40090, 0x3ffffff0 -- enumerated, all �0 mod 4) so the two semantics are indistinguishable there; sub-word is already disclosed; a sub-word row is NOT owed (the repair body tests no width) and should not be added while the semantics lack a warrant.

**(c)** Placement is per the ruling and the upstream hunk: writes inside the NULL check, exception outside (READ). Reachability: no guest-reachable refused access by a NULL-cmmu cpu on an unmodified machine (REASONED from S7: both CMMU machines attach both CMMUs to every started cpu; the CMMU-less machines have no reachable refusing device except testm88k's dev_fb.c:719-721, which P4 reads as fenced). NOT CHECKABLE: the exact predicate at dev_fb.c:719-721 versus the scan's routing of accesses that start inside but extend past the device end -- settling fact: those lines plus the device-scan endpoint test; if reachable, the check is crash-load-bearing on testm88k, not decoration. Either way it is licensed (upstream hunk, R13) and its variants are unobservable at rung 3 -- disclosed, not rowed: correct.

**(d)** No. The ported hunk is insert-only between the `debug()` and `m88k_exception`, replacing only the TODO comment (READ, 3.4); guard, vector expression, debug line and return are untouched.

**(e)** Path confirmed (REASONED from 8.1/8.2/S2/S10): the new body returns 0 without touching `cpu->running`, so :591-592 does not fire; res 0-1; guard taken; cmmu[1] gets 0x30000/0x3ffffff0; vector 3 with DMA0 = 0x3ffffff0 (& ~3 leaves it unchanged). The skipped tail is inert for stores, and its read-buffer write is inert for a failing load (buffer zeroed at :491-492; a failing load returns before writing its register). STORE: identical arm behavior. INSTRUCTION fetch at 0x3ffffff0: cmmu[0] now gets the metadata -- note HEAD's case writes cmmu[1] even for instruction accesses, a second wrong-CMMU wrong the fix removes -- vector 2; free-running the handler never runs (R1), so the instruction half rides R2 stepped (P5).

**(f)** P1: YES. READ (S16, 8.3): the arm compiles in cpu_m88k.c's TU via tmp_m88k_tail.c; CMMU_PFSR/PFAR/BERROR live only in m8820x.h; nothing in that TU includes it; upstream's own diff adds the include to cpu_m88k.c. So the ruled repair does not compile without a fourth edit. Upstream's placement is on record; an alternative that keeps three files is `#ifdef MEM_M88K #include "thirdparty/m8820x.h" #endif` at the top of memory_rw.c (same TU, same collision questions). NOT CHECKABLE: name collisions in cpu_m88k.c's macro namespace -- settling fact: a gate-1 build of the m88k unit; upstream's warrant transfers imperfectly since the fork has since edited cpu_m88k.c (79cd12e8). Either placement, the witness's "three files" baseline row (2.1) is stale and must be extended.

**(g)** P9: yes -- the three dev_m8820x.c records go stale the moment the arm writes PFSR/PFAR and the LUNA store disappears; one is already stale at HEAD (cites :614; the write is at :626 -- READ). Comment-only dev_m8820x.c edits in the same diff are right. The TODO :615-619 IS replaced -- the hunk's minus lines are exactly that comment (READ, 3.4).

**(h)** May claim: for a device-CLAIMED, device-REFUSED access on the two MC88200 machines, the data (and, stepped-only, instruction) CMMU now carry BERROR and the faulting physical address, with the exception unchanged; the LUNA probe address now faults instead of serving 0. Must disclaim: unclaimed addresses raise no bus error at all (R3/S3); on luna-88k every refusal except the probe case stops the cpu before the arm (S3) -- the generic arm is witnessed on mvme187; CMMU-less machines get no metadata and are unchanged; instruction-fetch refusals halt free-running (R1, unchanged); no_exceptions callers get no metadata; translation-fault PFSR/PFAR semantics unchanged; sub-word PFAR carries no local warrant. The ledger TITLE already scopes this correctly.

**(i)** P3: `kept` is right. REASONED: the only OpenBSD reader of the sibling is trap.c:405-411's two-BERROR veto; every translation fault resets the sibling first (S9), so enumerating the writer set, the differing combination {ipfsr=BERROR, dpfsr?BERROR at handler time} is unreachable -- kept-vs-reset cannot change what this guest does. Warrant: upstream's hunk does not clear the sibling, and the icmmu rows pin it with the reset-variant killed. The hardware question is unanswerable from the packet (READ, S12) and is correctly carried by the contract's sibling note. P8: the round changes guest-visible behavior at a documented OS-probe address (upstream's comment -- READ); whether the rig's guest reads 0x3ffffff0 is NOT CHECKABLE (no OpenBSD/luna88k source on disk; the rig image's OS is not named) -- a before/after boot-to-`login:` on the luna rig is the cheap settling measurement and is owed before the round ships.

**(j)** One unnamed member: (5) the luna house-style refusal (`cpu->running = 0; return 0;` in the case) -- behaviorally the twin of (4) via `default:`. It loses to (2): the address exists precisely to give the OS a catchable illegal-address fault (the case's own comment -- READ), and (2) is the dev_unreadable FAULT idiom, which R12 says works on M88K. (1) leaves the probe read succeeding; (3) is refused on record; (4) halts. Making the address unclaimed is not available (it is interior to the luna device's range, S19). The ruling's choice is correct.

## Q2 -- the owed rows

**(a)** Clause (a) is discharged by the witness (READ: RED on HEAD, PRESENT 8/8, controls green, binary from a src-identical commit). Post-fix half owed. Nothing else -- except the standing R2 warning: the pre-fix leg must never be recorded as clause (c)'s medium.

